# TypeScript

`any`, non-null assertions, floating promises, and `console` all fail `make lint`.

```ts
// ❌ BAD
const data = JSON.parse(body) as Payload;
const name = user!.name;
sendEmail(user);
console.log("saved", id);

// ✅ GOOD
const data = payloadSchema.parse(JSON.parse(body));
const name = user?.name ?? "unknown";
await sendEmail(user);
logger.info({ id }, "saved");
```

## Narrow unknown, do not assert

External input — request bodies, webhook payloads, third-party responses, parsed
JSON — is `unknown` until a Zod schema validates it. A cast moves the failure from
the boundary to somewhere unrelated and much later.

## Branded IDs

IDs are branded so they cannot be swapped:

```ts
// ❌ BAD — compiles, wrong at runtime
function getMembers(organizationId: string) {}
getMembers(user.id);

// ✅ GOOD — a type error
function getMembers(organizationId: OrganizationId) {}
```

## Other rules

- `import type { Foo }` for type-only imports.
- Money is an integer in minor units, never a float.
- No `process.env` outside `@repo/env`.
- Prefer `satisfies` over annotation when you want inference kept.
